task: benchmark-fairness-audit
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-06
branch: master (docs + one new benchmark class; measured, not refactored)

why: the operator asked whether the cats/ZIO/fs2 lanes are honest and
  optimal from THEIR side — "не хотелось бы самообманываться". Read
  every competitor lane against the library sources in the Coursier
  cache, then measured what the lanes pay that is not the thing under
  test (FairnessProbeBenchmark, 29 lanes).

found, same run, N as in the tables:
  - cats `unsafeRunSync` hops to the compute pool and blocks on a
    queue (IO.scala:1031): a 7.6us floor every cats lane pays and no
    okay lane does. kyo runAndBlock 7.3. ZIO/okay ~0. Small (3-5% of
    the cats rows) but never stated.
  - §1 bind chain: `IO(x+1)` is a Delay node, `Cont.Pure(x+1)` is
    not. With `IO.pure`: 147.5 -> 125.5. FIFTEEN PERCENT of the cats
    row is the node kind, not the runtime. ZIO Exit vs succeed: 5%.
  - §5 pipeline: ZIO from `iterate` 628; from `ZStream.range` 36.5.
    fs2 from `range` 1340-1729 (`range` is emit++go, a singleton per
    element, Stream.scala:3981); from `Stream.emits`, PURE, 23.3.
    The table prices both from their worst source against okay's
    best mode, and its own footnote admits the fair lanes were
    "not yet measured". fs2 overstated 60x, ZIO 17x.
  - §6 merge: fs2 chunked BEFORE the merge 303 vs singletons 35700
    (N=2000). The table's fs2 row is the singleton spelling only.
  - ZIO queue consumer: `Ref.update` 111.5, a plain var 105.5,
    idiomatic `runSum` 152.6 — the project's spelling is NOT
    penalising ZIO. Verified, no change.
  - cats producer via fs2 evalMap vs an IO loop: 3666 vs 3233, bars
    overlap — not the cause of the cats queue row. Verified.

caveat on the run: one other sbt was alive when it started (load
  3.1). Lane-to-lane inside the run is what the findings rest on; the
  numbers going INTO the tables are re-measured quiet before landing.
